{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/plan.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tspec: 2026-10-13-customer-wishlist\n     3\t---\n     4\t## Approach\n     5\t\n     6\tImplement `src/wishlist.ts` with a module-local Map keyed by `customerId` and a per-customer Map keyed by product ID.\n     7\tExpose synchronous `saveWishlistItem`, `getWishlist` and `removeWishlistItem` operations and their types through `src/index.ts`.\n     8\tStore only product IDs and first-save timestamps; use the existing catalogue lookup to validate saves and resolve current details on reads.\n     9\tPreserve duplicate saves, sort reads newest first and keep every operation within the addressed customer's wishlist.\n    10\tUse `bun:test` claim coverage with explicit dates and distinct customers; retain the [model sketch](reference/data-model.md) and [contract draft](reference/module-design.md) as working material.\n    11\t\n    12\t## Trade-offs\n    13\t\n    14\tSanne chose process-local storage and exported module operations over persistence or a UI, as recorded in the [accepted decision](../../decisions/2026-10-13-wishlist-module-and-storage.md). This keeps the existing setup and loses wishlist state on restart.\n    15\t\n    16\t## Files\n    17\t\n    18\t```text\n    19\tsrc/\n    20\t  wishlist.ts      new: store, types and save/view/remove operations\n    21\t  index.ts         changed: re-export wishlist functions and types\n    22\ttest/\n    23\t  wishlist.test.ts new: claim coverage and public export verification\n    24\t```\n    25\t\n    26\t## Architecture to update at ship\n    27\t\n    28\t- `docs/architecture/data-model.md`, from [reference/data-model.md](reference/data-model.md).\n    29\t- `docs/architecture/boundaries.md`, from [reference/module-design.md](reference/module-design.md).\n    30\t- `docs/architecture/contracts.md`, from [reference/module-design.md](reference/module-design.md).\n    31\t- `docs/architecture/stack.md`, from [reference/module-design.md](reference/module-design.md).\n    32\t\n    33\t## Risks\n    34\t\n    35\t- A repeated save could replace the original time or move an item; `preserve-first-save` and `newest-first` prove the ordering.\n    36\t- Shared module state could mix customers; `isolate-wishlists` and `isolate-removal` prove separation, with distinct test customers preventing fixture overlap.\n    37\t- Stored product snapshots could return old prices; `current-product-details` proves that reads use the current catalogue.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
